No—you don’t ask them to vote on whether they deserve dignity. **You take a position and give them a voice in how you act on it.** Leadership isn’t unilateral detonation, but it also isn’t hiding behind a committee.

Go in with a recommendation:

> “We were moved because Marta says our synth and quarterstaff elements don’t suit the Heritage audience. Those elements were known when we were booked. I consider the late change disrespectful to this production and to the work you’ve contributed. My position is that we demand reinstatement under the original conditions by **[deadline]**. If the organizers refuse, I intend to withdraw the show and explain the reason accurately and publicly. Before I make that final decision, I want to hear anything important I may be overlooking—but I’m not asking you to decide whether this treatment was acceptable. I don’t believe it was.”

That shows backbone because you’re:

- naming the stated reason rather than sanitizing it;
- defending the legitimacy of the work;
- proposing a clear demand and consequence;
- taking responsibility for the final call;
- allowing affected people to flag costs you may not know about.

One important precision: only say Marta called you “not proper folk” if those were actually her words. If she said “too loud for the proper folk crowd,” quote that and then say **you regard the decision as gatekeeping**. Don’t accidentally give her an easy factual rebuttal.

And if someone asks, “Are we really prepared to walk?” you can answer:

> “Yes. I won’t accept a last-minute demotion based on an artistic purity test. I’m giving them one opportunity to honor the booking; otherwise, we leave together.”

That’s not putting dignity to a vote. It’s setting a boundary without treating your cast like props in your confrontation.
